\begin{sample}{Fluctuations of the first two zeros}
Find the smooth zeros $s_1$ and $s_2$, the local mean spacings there from \cref{eq:intro-gap}, and the fluctuations $(t_n - s_n)/\gap_n$ of the first two zeros.

\textbf{Step 1: rewrite the condition.} With $u = t/2\pi$, \cref{eq:intro-Nsm} reads $\Nsm = u\log u - u + \tfrac78$. The condition $\Nsm(s_1) = 1 - \tfrac12 = \tfrac12$ becomes $u\log u - u = \tfrac12 - \tfrac78 = -0.375$. Call the left side $f(u)$.

\textbf{Step 2: bracket by trial.} $f(2.30) = 2.30\times 0.832909 - 2.30 = -0.3843$, below $-0.375$; $f(2.32) = 2.32\times 0.841567 - 2.32 = -0.3676$, above it. The function $f$ increases for $u > 1$, because its derivative is $\log u + 1 - 1 = \log u > 0$. So the solution lies between $2.30$ and $2.32$.

\textbf{Step 3: refine.} $f(2.311) = 2.311\times 0.837680 - 2.311 = -0.3751$, short of $-0.375$ by $0.00012$. Near $u = 2.311$ the slope of $f$ is $\log u = 0.838$, so $u$ must grow by a further $0.00012/0.838 = 0.00015$, giving $u = 2.31115$. (Chapter~\ref{ch:asymptotics} turns this step into Newton's method.)

\textbf{Step 4: $s_1$ and its spacing.} $s_1 = 2\pi u = 6.2831853\times 2.31115 = 14.5214$. The mean spacing is $2\pi/\log 2.31115 = 6.2831853/0.83775 = 7.500$.

\textbf{Step 5: fluctuation of zero 1.} $(14.134725 - 14.5214)/7.500 = -0.3867/7.500 = -0.0516$.

\textbf{Step 6: zero 2.} Now $f(u) = \tfrac32 - \tfrac78 = 0.625$. Trials: $f(3.28) = 3.28\times1.187843 - 3.28 = 0.6161$ (below), $f(3.29) = 3.29\times 1.190887 - 3.29 = 0.6280$ (above), $f(3.2875) = 0.6250$ to four decimals; the same refining step gives $u = 3.28746$. Then $s_2 = 6.2831853\times 3.28746 = 20.6557$, the spacing is $6.2831853/\log 3.28746 = 6.2831853/1.19012 = 5.2795$, and the fluctuation is $(21.022040 - 20.6557)/5.2795 = 0.3663/5.2795 = 0.0694$.

\textbf{Answer:}
\[
\boxed{s_1 = 14.5214,\ \text{fluct}_1 = -0.052;\qquad s_2 = 20.6557,\ \text{fluct}_2 = +0.069}
\]
The first zero sits a little below its smooth position, the second a little above; both displacements are a small fraction of one spacing. The library call \texttt{qmnt.fluct(qmnt.zeros(2), np.array([1, 2]))} returns the same two numbers.
\end{sample}

